---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getExperience, experienceFrame } from '../../../data/experiences';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths';
import Term from '../../../components/Term.astro';
import TrainerLaunch from '../../../components/TrainerLaunch.astro';
import { RUN } from '../../../lessons/instrument-flight/ils-trainer-model.mjs';
import { levelTrim } from '../../../lessons/instrument-flight/attitude-model.mjs';
import '../../../styles/instrument-flight.css';
const exercise = getExperience('ils-approach');
const mod = exercise.module;
const trainerHref = sitePath(experienceFrame(exercise));
const attitude = sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/attitude-instrument-flying/');
const procedures = sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/instrument-procedures/');
const core = [
 ['example', 'One approach, start to finish'], ['ils', 'What the ILS gives you'], ['needles', 'Reading the needles'],
 ['setup', 'Before the localizer'], ['intercept', 'Intercept and the wind'], ['tracking', 'Tracking both needles'],
 ['decision', 'Limits and the decision at the DA'], ['missed', 'Going missed'], ['recap', 'Key points'], ['launch', 'Practice: the hand-flown ILS'],
];
const reference = [['equipment', 'Range, markers and failures'], ['model', 'What the exercise models'], ['sims', 'In the sim'], ['check', 'Check your understanding'], ['sources', 'Sources and applicability']];
// [1] AFMAN 11-217 Vol 1 (2010) by printed page; [2] CNATRA P-1204 by
// printed page, for the needle scaling and the ILS technique only; [3] the
// DCS: A-10C II flight manual, sim crossover only.
const pp = (n: number) => (page: string) => `[${n}, ${/[–,]/.test(page) ? 'pp.' : 'p.'} ${page}]`;
const af = pp(1), t45 = pp(2), dcs = pp(3);
const da = RUN.tdze + RUN.da, locMins = RUN.tdze + RUN.locMins;
const descent = levelTrim(RUN.speed, { dragK: RUN.dragK, gamma: -3 }), level = levelTrim(RUN.speed, { dragK: RUN.dragK });
const r0 = (v: number) => Math.round(v);
---
<SiteLayout title={mod.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="instrument-flight" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Instrument Flight',href:sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/')},{label:mod.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={exercise.manualId} data-module-revision={mod.revision}>
  <h2>{mod.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {mod.revision} · The ILS from the start of the approach to the decision altitude: what the localizer and glide slope show, setting up, the intercept and the wind, tracking both needles, the limits, the decision and the missed approach. Written for fixed-wing military pilots of every service, from the Air Force's instrument flight procedures manual, with the Navy's instrument training instruction for the needle scaling.</p>
  <p>It assumes the crosscheck from <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a>; the approach parts it shares with other approaches (the FAF, minimums and the plate) are in <a href={`${procedures}#approach`}>Instrument Procedures</a>. Then hand-fly it in the trainer, which opens in its own window.</p>
 </section>
 <TrainerLaunch variant="jump" experienceId={exercise.id} label="Fly the ILS by hand in the trainer" />
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <div class="contents-groups">
   <div><h3>The core sequence</h3><ol>{core.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
   <div><h3>Reference</h3><ol>{reference.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
  </div>
 </nav>

 <p class="lede">The ILS is the standard precision approach: two radio beams, one for the course and one for the descent, that bring you down to a couple of hundred feet above the runway in cloud. Flown by hand, it is the crosscheck under pressure. The needles are angles, so they grow more sensitive all the way down, and the wind you were not told about pushes you off the localizer from the moment you intercept it. At the end you have a few seconds to decide whether to land or go missed. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a></p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to:</p>
 <ol class="outcomes">
  <li id="o1">read the localizer and glide slope needles, and explain why they grow more sensitive close in;</li>
  <li id="o2">set up for the approach and intercept the localizer and find the heading that holds it in an unknown wind;</li>
  <li id="o3">capture the glide slope and hold both needles within one dot with small attitude and power changes;</li>
  <li id="o4">recognise the needle limits that end an approach, and go missed when one is broken;</li>
  <li id="o5">make the land or go-missed decision at the DA, and fly the start of the missed approach.</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="example">One approach, start to finish</h2>
 <p>An invented approach, flown the way the trainer flies it. Final course 090, DA {da} ft ({RUN.da} ft above the touchdown zone), {RUN.speed} knots, gear and flaps down.</p>
 <ol class="exchange">
  <li><span class="who">Set up</span><span class="said">ILS tuned and its identifier heard, final course 090 set on the HSI, the missed approach briefed. Gear and flaps down and the landing checklist done before the glide slope arrives. <a href="#setup" class="step">Before the localizer</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Start</span><span class="said">Level at 1,200 ft above the field, 4 NM out, heading 065: the course bar is one dot right and the glide slope pointer one dot above centre, so you are left of course and below the glide slope. <a href="#intercept" class="step">Intercept</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Intercept</span><span class="said">The bar starts to move. Roll out on 090 as it centres. It drifts left again: a crosswind from the left. Turn 5° left, to 085, and the bar stops. That is the heading that holds the course. <a href="#intercept" class="step">The wind</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Glide slope</span><span class="said">The pointer comes down to the centre. Lower the nose about 3° and take off a little power, and the VSI settles near 700 ft a minute. <a href="#tracking" class="step">Tracking</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Final</span><span class="said">Half a dot above the glide slope (the pointer half a dot low): ease the nose down a degree, then put it back as the pointer returns. Bar a quarter dot right: 2° right, then back. Each correction smaller than the last as you get closer. <a href="#tracking" class="step">Tracking</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">DA</span><span class="said">Approach lights in sight, both needles inside one dot: land. Still in cloud, or more than a dot off: go missed. <a href="#decision" class="step">Decision</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Missed</span><span class="said">Power up, climb straight ahead on the published missed approach, and tell ATC. <a href="#missed" class="step">Going missed</a></span></li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="ils">What the ILS gives you</h2>
 <p>The ILS has two parts on the ground. The localizer beside the far end of the runway gives the course, left or right of the centerline; the glide slope beside the touchdown zone gives the descent path, usually about 3°. Range comes from marker beacons, DME or a fix on another aid. An approach needs the localizer, the glide slope and the outer marker or its published substitute: without the glide slope it becomes a localizer approach with higher minimums, and without the localizer it is not authorised. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a></p>
 <p>Because it guides you down the glidepath, the ILS is a precision approach: it ends at a <Term id="Decision altitude">decision altitude</Term> (DA), where you land or go missed, not at a level-off altitude and missed approach point as a non-precision approach does. <a href="#source-afman">{af('206–208')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> course from the localizer, descent from the glide slope, range from markers or DME, and a decision at the DA.</p>

 <h2 id="needles">Reading the needles</h2>
 <p>The localizer drives the HSI's course bar, and the glide slope a pointer beside the card; many aircraft also show both as crossed bars on the attitude indicator. Fly toward the needle: bar right, the course is to your right; glide slope pointer high, the glide slope is above you. Full deflection is two dots. It is an angle, not a distance: about 2.5° either side of a 5°-wide localizer course, and 0.7° above or below the glide slope. <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a> <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a> So the same error in feet moves the needles further the closer you get, and a correction that was right far out is too big near minimums. Figure 1 shows it.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson panel-fig" data-lesson="ils" aria-labelledby="ils-title">
  <h3 id="ils-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — ILS needles</h3>
  <p>Move the aircraft along the approach, off the centerline and off the glide slope, and watch the HSI and the attitude indicator's ILS bars. Or fly a whole approach: it starts right of course and low, captures the localizer, then the glide slope.</p>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="ils-range">Range <input type="range" id="ils-range" name="range" min="0.5" max="10" step="0.1" value="6" /> <output for="range">6.0 NM</output></label>
   <label for="ils-right">Off centerline <input type="range" id="ils-right" name="right" min="-2000" max="2000" step="20" value="400" /> <output for="right">400 ft right</output></label>
   <label for="ils-above">Off glide slope <input type="range" id="ils-above" name="above" min="-400" max="400" step="10" value="-60" /> <output for="above">60 ft low</output></label>
  </div>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-fly>Fly the approach</button> <button type="button" data-centre>Centre the needles</button></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll"><svg class="inst" viewBox="0 0 700 470" role="img" aria-label="Plan view and profile of an ILS approach beside an HSI and an attitude indicator showing the localizer and glide slope needles"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Example: 400 ft right of the centerline at 6 NM is just over half a dot, with the course bar left of centre; the same 400 ft at 1 NM is more than one dot.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Schematic. Needle scaling from the Navy's training instruction (2.5° and 0.7° full scale) and the Air Force manual's dots; the 3° glide slope, 8,000 ft runway, antenna positions and 200 ft DA are invented for the figure, and real installations vary. Both views are stretched across the approach so the offsets show.</p>
 </section>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> fly toward the needles, and remember they show angles: a dot is hundreds of feet far out and much less close in.</p>

 <h2 id="setup">Before the localizer</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Setting up <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210, 249')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-60')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td>Early</td><td>Tune the ILS, identify it, and keep monitoring the identifier; set the published front course, even for a back course approach</td></tr>
   <tr><td>Before starting</td><td>Know the missed approach: where it goes and what it climbs to</td></tr>
   <tr><td>To the localizer</td><td>Fly the published procedure or the vectors on the navigation aid it uses, backed up by any other aid you have</td></tr>
   <tr><td>Before glide slope intercept</td><td>Configured, on speed, landing checklist done, so nothing is left for the descent</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> tuned, identified, briefed and configured before the glide slope arrives.</p>

 <h2 id="intercept">Intercept and the wind</h2>
 <p>On the localizer, correct with heading changes of no more than 5° either side of the wind-corrected heading. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-60, 6-62')}</a></p>
 <p>The wind-corrected heading is the one that keeps the course bar still, not centred: find it by flying the course heading, watching which way the bar drifts, and taking a few degrees into the wind until it stops. Then centre the bar with a small turn and come back to the corrected heading. For the drift angle in degrees, divide the crosswind by your true airspeed in miles a minute: 12 knots of crosswind at 140 knots (about 2.3 NM a minute) is about 5°. <a href="#source-afman">{af('157–158')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> hold a heading that stops the bar moving, then make small corrections either side of it.</p>

 <h2 id="tracking">Tracking both needles</h2>
 <p>Fly toward the needles: course bar right, the course is to your right; glide slope pointer high, the glide slope is above you. Full deflection is two dots, about 2.5° either side of the localizer course and 0.7° above or below the glide slope. <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a> <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a> Because they are angles, one dot is over 700 ft sideways at 4 NM but under 300 ft at the DA in the exercise's geometry, so a correction that was right far out is too big near minimums.</p>
 <p>As the glide slope pointer centres, set the descent attitude and power to stay on speed and on the glidepath. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-62')}</a> For a 3° glidepath the vertical speed is about ground speed × 10 ÷ 2: at 140 knots of ground speed, 700 ft a minute; more with a tailwind, less into a headwind. <a href="#source-afman">{af('208')}</a> At the final approach fix, start the clock: if the glide slope fails you may, depending on the minimums, continue as a localizer approach and time to the missed approach point. <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-62')}</a> Make each change on the attitude indicator first, then let the performance instruments show whether it was enough, as in <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> small attitude changes, held, then checked; and smaller ones the closer you get.</p>

 <h2 id="decision">Limits and the decision at the DA</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>When an ILS ends <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210, 250')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Rule</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td>Anywhere on final</td><td>Full-scale localizer deflection, or an unreliable localizer</td><td>Discontinue the approach</td></tr>
   <tr><td>Below localizer minimums</td><td>More than one dot (half scale) below, or two dots (full scale) above, the glide slope</td><td>Do not descend below localizer minimums; recapture within limits and you may continue to the DA</td></tr>
   <tr><td>At the DA</td><td>Runway environment in sight and in position to land safely</td><td>Continue to land</td></tr>
   <tr><td>At the DA</td><td>No required visual references</td><td>Go missed, immediately</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The runway environment is any one of the approach lights, threshold, runway end identifier lights, touchdown zone, runway or its markings and lights, or the visual approach slope indicator; on the approach lights alone, not below 100 ft above the touchdown zone unless the red termination or side row bars are visible. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209')}</a> "In position to land safely" has no number in the source. The exercise draws the line at one dot on both needles, which is its own rule.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> at the DA there are only two answers, and the default is the missed approach.</p>

 <h2 id="missed">Going missed</h2>
 <p>On an ILS the missed approach starts at the DA. Go missed immediately if you reach it, or are below it, without the required visual references, and fly the published missed approach unless ATC has given you another. Its obstacle clearance assumes you start at the DA and climb at least 200 ft per nautical mile. If you go missed early, fly the approach course to the missed approach point at or above the DA before turning, unless ATC clears otherwise. <a href="#source-afman">{af('249–250')}</a> The rest of the missed approach, including practice approaches, is in <a href={`${procedures}#missed`}>Instrument Procedures</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> no visual references at the DA, go missed at once; early, stay on the course to the missed approach point before turning.</p>

 <h2 id="recap">Key points</h2>
 <ul class="recap">
  <li>Tune, identify, brief the missed approach and configure before the glide slope arrives.</li>
  <li>Find the heading that stops the course bar moving, and correct no more than 5° either side of it.</li>
  <li>The needles are angles: the same correction moves them further the closer you are.</li>
  <li>Full-scale localizer on final means discontinue; below localizer minimums, stay within one dot low and two dots high.</li>
  <li>At the DA, land only with the runway environment in sight and in position to land; otherwise go missed at once, straight ahead on the published missed approach.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="launch">Practice: the hand-flown ILS</h2>
 <section class="status-panel" data-experience-id={exercise.id} data-experience-revision={exercise.revision}>
  <h3>{exercise.title}</h3><p>{exercise.maturity} · Exercise revision {exercise.revision}</p>
  <p>{exercise.applicability} Prerequisites: {exercise.prerequisites.join(' · ')}.</p>
  <p>The trainer flies the core sequence above, from the intercept to the DA. Each run starts level below the glide slope, off the localizer, on an intercept heading, with a random wind you are not told. Fly both needles to the DA on the panel from <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a>, then land or go missed. The debrief scores the time within one dot, the worst deviations, any limit broken and the decision, and replays the flown path on a plan and profile view beside the panel.</p>
  <p>Controls: the arrow keys move the attitude (<kbd>↑</kbd> nose down, <kbd>↓</kbd> nose up, <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> bank), <kbd>W</kbd> and <kbd>S</kbd> the power, <kbd>G</kbd> goes missed and <kbd>L</kbd> lands at the DA. An optional <strong>Auto power</strong> switch holds the speed for you, so you can learn to fly the glide slope with pitch alone before adding the power. A joystick or HOTAS can be assigned to the stick and throttle, and a game controller works with its left stick and triggers, and on a phone or with a mouse there are buttons under the panel. No joystick is needed.</p>
  <TrainerLaunch href={trainerHref} experienceId={exercise.id} label="Launch the ILS trainer" />
 </section>

 <h2 id="equipment">Range, markers and failures</h2>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Range.</strong> The localizer is usable to 18 miles within 10° of the centerline unless the plate says otherwise; the glide slope to 10 miles, more where it has been certified for it. <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-57')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>False glide slopes.</strong> A false glide slope signal can appear with everything working and no flags. Confirm your position with other navigation aids where you can. <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Marker beacons.</strong> Most ILS approaches do not have all three, and some none; without them, cross-radial fixes, DME or radar give range. The outer marker usually marks the FAF and often the glidepath intercept; the middle marker is near the DA for a Category I approach, roughly half a mile out and 200 ft above the ground; the inner marker is the Category II DA, at about 100 ft. <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-59')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Failures.</strong> Glide slope lost: a localizer approach, if its minimums allow, timed from the FAF. Localizer unreliable or full scale on final: discontinue. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a></li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="model">What the exercise models</h2>
 <p>The needles use the same scaling as <a href="#needles">Figure 1</a>: 2.5° and 0.7° full scale, two dots each way, a 3° glide slope. The limits and the decision follow the Air Force manual. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Everything else is invented: the aircraft, a generic jet that holds the attitude you set and needs about {r0(level.power)}% power level and {r0(descent.power)}% on the glide slope at {RUN.speed} knots, gear and flaps down; the field elevation of {RUN.tdze} ft; the DA of {da} ft ({RUN.da} ft above the touchdown zone) and localizer minimums of {locMins} ft; winds up to {RUN.windKt[1]} knots from any direction; the runway environment being in sight at the DA on about {r0(RUN.visualChance * 100)}% of runs; and "in position to land" meaning within one dot on both needles. True airspeed is taken as indicated, there is no turbulence, and the run pauses at the DA for the decision, which in the aircraft is immediate, then ends there: the landing and the missed approach itself are not flown.</p>
 

 <section class="sim-panel" aria-labelledby="sims">
  <h2 id="sims">In the sim</h2>
  <p>The DCS: A-10C II manual covers flying an ILS; this panel is from that manual only. Other DCS aircraft and Falcon BMS were not checked for this revision.</p>
  <h3>Flying an ILS in DCS</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
   <li>Turn on the ILS panel and set the station's frequency (108.10 to 111.95 MHz); the CDU DIVERT page lists airfield ILS frequencies. Press ILS on the navigation mode select panel. <a href="#source-a10">{dcs('573')}</a></li>
   <li>Fly the localizer and glide slope bars on the ADI to a cross; the marker light and tone show the outer and inner markers. Some runways, the manual's example is Batumi, have one landing direction and no beacons. <a href="#source-a10">{dcs('573–574, 602–603')}</a></li>
   <li>The manual's ILS starts at 2,000 ft above the ground, flaps up, around 150 KIAS, and configures (speed brakes 40%, gear, flaps down) over the outer marker. <a href="#source-a10">{dcs('602')}</a></li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Where it differs from the real-world sources</h3>
  <ul>
   <li><strong>Glide slope limits.</strong> The manual's "general rule" is to go missed more than 1 dot low or 2 dots high. <a href="#source-a10">{dcs('574')}</a> In the Air Force manual the same numbers are a limit on descending below localizer minimums, and recapturing within them lets you continue. <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a></li>
   <li><strong>Configuring.</strong> The manual configures over the outer marker; the Air Force manual and the Navy instruction want you configured before glide slope intercept, which is often at the outer marker or before it. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-59–6-60')}</a></li>
   <li>Not described in the manual, so not claimed either way: the 5° localizer correction limit, the descent-rate rule and the runway environment list.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>To practise:</strong> fly an ILS in the sim with the real limits above, keeping localizer corrections within 5° of the wind-corrected heading and briefing the missed approach before you start. The trainer above practises the part every sim shares: flying the needles by hand to the DA.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">1</span>At 8 NM, half a dot of localizer took a 5° heading correction to fix. At 1 NM you see half a dot again. Same correction?</summary><p>No. The needle shows an angle, so half a dot at 1 NM is a much smaller distance off the centerline than at 8 NM. Make a smaller correction, and keep it within 5° of the wind-corrected heading.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">1</span>The glide slope pointer is below centre. Are you high or low?</summary><p>High: the glide slope is below you. Fly toward the needle.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">2</span>The glide slope fails half way down final. What now?</summary><p>The approach becomes a localizer approach, if its minimums allow: use its MDA and time from the FAF to the missed approach point.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">2</span>You fly the final course heading and the course bar drifts steadily right. Where is the wind from, and what do you do?</summary><p>From the right. Turn a few degrees right of the course to find the heading that stops the bar, centre it with a small turn, then hold the corrected heading.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">3</span>Half a dot above the glide slope at 4 NM took a 1° nose-down change. Why is the same change too much at 1 NM?</summary><p>The needles are angles: a dot is far fewer feet close in, so the same change moves the pointer much further and faster. Make smaller, quicker corrections near minimums.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">4</span>Below localizer minimums the glide slope pointer sits one and a half dots above centre: the glide slope is above you. May you keep descending?</summary><p>No. You are more than one dot low; do not descend below localizer minimums until you are back within limits.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">4</span>The course bar pegs at full scale on final. What now?</summary><p>Discontinue the approach and go missed.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">5</span>At the DA the approach lights are in sight, but the course bar is a dot and a half right. Land?</summary><p>In the exercise, no: it counts as not in position to land. In real flying the source asks for a position from which a safe landing is possible, which a dot and a half off near the DA is unlikely to give.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">5</span>You decide to go missed a mile before the DA. Can you turn onto the missed approach heading at once?</summary><p>No: unless ATC clears otherwise, fly the approach to the missed approach point at or above the DA before turning.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <p id="source-afman"><strong>[1] US Air Force, AFMAN 11-217 Volume 1, Instrument Flight Procedures, 22 October 2010.</strong> <a href="https://www.code7700.com/pdfs/usaf/afman11-217v1.pdf">Read on code7700.com's copy</a>, 2 November 2026, by printed page: holding drift (pp. 157–158), precision and non-precision approaches and descent rate (pp. 206–208), below minimums and the ILS (pp. 209–210), missed approach (pp. 249–250). Superseded by the consolidated AFMAN 11-217 of 10 June 2019, which could not be retrieved; numbers may have changed.</p>
 <p id="source-t45"><strong>[2] Chief of Naval Air Training, CNATRA P-1204 (Rev. 09-16, Change 2 of 25 March 2021), Flight Training Instruction, Instrument, T-45C MPTS and IUT.</strong> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20211009235549/https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/local/docs/pat-pubs/P-1204.pdf">Internet Archive copy of cnatra.navy.mil</a> (October 2021 snapshot), read 2 November 2026 by printed page: ILS needle scaling (p. 1-2), localizer and glide slope range (p. 6-57), marker beacons (p. 6-59), configuring before glide slope intercept (p. 6-60) and localizer corrections and the FAF (p. 6-62). A training command instruction for one aircraft type, cited for these generic points only.</p>
 <p id="source-a10"><strong>[3] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: A-10C II Tank Killer Flight Manual (English), July 2023.</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/dcs-warthog_2_flight_manual_en/">Download page</a>, read 2 November 2026, pp. 573–574 and 602–603. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page excerpts are in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/instrument-flight-evidence.md">Instrument flight evidence</a>.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: generic instrument procedures for fixed-wing pilots of every service. Your service's instrument manual, the aircraft's flight manual and the published approach govern real approaches, their minimums and their limits. Figure 1, the worked approach and the exercise's aircraft and approach are invented. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
